# Isolated negative controls: auditor-stall-watchdog worker-teardown assertion
#
# Requirement under test: a cancelled detached worker must be observed GONE by
# the parent. The assertion must (a) accept the default SIGTERM disposition
# when the watchdog fires before the child installs its handler, and (b) NOT
# accept a reaped job-scratch directory as proof, because production reaps that
# directory after its bounded termination waits whether or not the child died.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONTROL A (auditor-requested shape): signaling DISABLED, scratch cleanup
# INTACT. This is the case the previous control missed.
#
# Disposable copy /tmp/glla-ctl (since removed) modified ONLY terminateWorker
# to never call signalWorkerTree (no SIGTERM, no SIGKILL escalation), leaving
# removeAuditJobDirectory() fully intact at line 2925.
#
#   error: timed out waiting for: the detached silent worker was cancelled
#          (parent observed its exit)
#   (fail) stall: worker that never emits a first event is failed fast, not
#          left for the wall [16953.49ms]
#    0 pass
#    1 fail
#
# RESULT: correctly FAILS. The job directory WAS removed by the intact cleanup,
# yet the test still fails, proving directory absence is no longer sufficient
# evidence of teardown.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONTROL B (earlier, coarser): signaling AND scratch cleanup both disabled.
#
#   error: timed out waiting for: the detached silent worker was cancelled
#          (handler ran, or the job was reaped)
#   (fail) stall: worker that never emits a first event is failed fast, not
#          left for the wall [22638.19ms]
#    0 pass
#    1 fail
#
# RESULT: also fails, as expected. CONTROL A is the stronger case because the
# directory really is gone and only real process liveness distinguishes the
# outcomes.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Live source in the repository passes the same tests (11 passed, 0 failed),
# and the two tests dropped from ~32s to ~6s because they no longer wait out a
# handler that was never installed.
